{
  "requirement": "If the accepted result cannot be deserialized into `R`, awaiting the builder\nreturns `ValidationError::Agent`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.judge.submission.decoding` has one obligation. If the accepted `submit_result` value cannot be deserialized into `R`, awaiting the builder returns `ValidationError::Agent`.\n\n**Where decoding happens.** In `crates/trust-but-verify/src/procedure.rs` (`impl[Ask]#2/fn[execute]`), the value returned by the agent run goes through `serde_json::from_value(value)`. A failure is mapped to `ValidationError::Agent`.\n\n**What the harness accepts.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tools.rs#fn[submitted_value]` checks only the envelope. The object must have exactly one key, `result`. It does not check the result against the schema. So a well-formed envelope holding a value of the wrong type is accepted by the harness and reaches the decoding step.\n\n**Test coverage.** `typed_submission_references_resolve_and_invalid_results_are_agent_errors` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It covers both sides of the obligation:\n- **Success path.** `ask::<Answer>()` with `{\"result\":{\"decision\":\"Yes\"}}` decodes to `Decision::Yes`.\n- **Failure path.** The loop runs `ask::<bool>()` against one-turn scripts and asserts `Err(ValidationError::Agent(_))`. The case `{\"result\":\"wrong\"}` is the decoding case. It has a valid envelope, so the harness accepts it, and a string cannot deserialize into `bool`.\n- **Other cases.** The other three loop cases (`{}`, `{\"other\":true}`, and an extra key) are envelope rejections. They belong to `submission.invalid`, not to this requirement.\n\nThe assertion checks the specific `Agent` variant, so a different error type or a successful return would fail the test.\n\n**Other tests.** `invalid_typed_submission_is_an_agent_error` in `graph/validation_tests.rs` is marked for the parent `submission` requirement, not for the decoding requirement. It is not needed for adequacy.\n\n**Gaps.** The requirement has no further input classes. A mismatch between the accepted value and `R` always takes the same `from_value` branch, so more mismatch examples would not expose different required behavior. I found no gaps and no applicable `{fixme}`.",
  "references": "e3a5fe45d43bd8249c3bba6c58efc85b15e9af5ed286059fe88369f67025424e",
